+### [FPGA-based Raycasting](8615-fpga-based-raycasting.md)
+FPGA-based raycasting refers to specialized hardware architectures, specifically the UVIZARD and VIZARD II systems developed at the University of Tübingen, designed to accelerate perspective volume raycasting on custom PCI boards. Unlike general-purpose GPU solutions that emerged later, these systems rely on long pre-processing and fixed-function pipelines to achieve real-time performance for large datasets. It is distinguished by its implementation of early ray-termination and table-based gradient lookup on field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs).
 
 ### [Single Scattering Illumination](9628-single-scattering-illumination.md)
-A volumetric lighting model computing radiance from light undergoing exactly one scattering event. It distinguishes between Direct Single Scattering (DSS) and Indirect Single Scattering (ISS), providing analytic formulations for both, including occlusion handling via shadow volumes for real-time hardware rendering.
+Single Scattering Illumination is a volumetric lighting model that computes the radiance contribution of light undergoing exactly one scattering event within a participating medium. It solves the problem of realistic volumetric lighting by distinguishing between light scattered along the view ray (Direct Single Scattering, DSS) and light scattered along illumination rays from scene points (Indirect Single Scattering, ISS). Unlike basic volume rendering which often ignores volumetric shadows or uses approximations, this method provides an analytic formulation for both direct and indirect contributions, including occlusion handling via shadow volumes, enabling real-time hardware rendering of complex volumetric lighting effects.
 
 ### [Distributed Volume Raycasting with Bricks](9869-distributed-volume-raycasting-with-bricks.md)
-Subdivides volumetric data into convex subvolumes (bricks) assigned to separate cluster nodes for independent GPU raycasting. It uses software-based compositing to handle final image assembly, avoiding hardware blending limitations and `glReadPixels` bottlenecks.
