 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of pixels (rays). The complexity is $O(N_{pixels} \cdot N_{samples})$ [S1, unofficial][S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Proportional to the size of the 3D volume texture and 1D transfer function textures stored in GPU memory [S2][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Specific performance metrics vary by hardware. For example, an FPGA-based predecessor achieved 10 Hz for a $256^3$ volume [S6, unofficial], while modern GPU implementations can achieve interactive rates depending on resolution and volume size [S2][S6, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of GPU cores and memory bandwidth. Multi-core CPUs and clustered graphics boards can offer near-linear scaling [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** High parallelism, leverages existing graphics hardware, avoids complex visibility ordering [S2][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited by GPU memory capacity for large datasets, potential accuracy issues with ray-triangle intersections in unstructured grids, and fixed-function pipeline limitations in older APIs [S5, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Unsuitable for extremely large datasets that exceed GPU memory, or when high-precision geometric accuracy is critical for unstructured grids with complex topology [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of samples per ray and the number of pixels. The loop runs $t_{Max}$ times or until early termination [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the 3D volume texture and 1D transfer function texture in GPU memory [S2][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Performance is heavily dependent on the efficiency of the fragment shader and the texture memory bandwidth. Early ray termination (checking $remainOpacity < 0.0039$) significantly reduces average computation time for sparse volumes [S2].
+*   **Scaling:** Scaling is close to linear with multi-core CPUs if ray tracing is implemented there, but on GPUs, it scales with the number of parallel fragments processed [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Leverages the extremely parallel architecture of GPUs; avoids complex visibility order computations required by other methods [S3, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires all data to be in GPU memory, limiting dataset size [S5, unofficial]. Accuracy limitations of ray-triangle intersections can lower image quality [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Suitable for real-time visualization of moderate-sized volumetric datasets where hardware acceleration is available [S4, S5, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Unsuitable for extremely large datasets that exceed GPU memory capacity unless streaming techniques are employed [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-GPU Raycasting was introduced by Kruger in 2003, making it feasible from a computational standpoint due to advances in graphics hardware and algorithms [S4, unofficial]. Prior to this, performance constraints limited the practical application of illustrative visualization techniques [S4, unofficial]. The method leverages the parallel processing capabilities of GPUs to accelerate the ray casting process, which was previously computationally expensive on CPUs [S2][S4, unofficial].
